# t1048 — the CoW `&`-route leaks EVERY superseded buffer.
#
# `grow(&s)` reallocates `s` 64 times. Each CoW step allocates a new buffer
# and abandons the old one: `string_cow=64`, `total_frees=65`, and
# `live_bytes=32704` at exit. The same 64 appends written INLINE
# (`s = s + "..."` in main) free as they go and end at `live_bytes=0`.
#
# NO SLICE IS INVOLVED — this reproduces with a plain String and nothing
# else, which is what separates it from the `t0871` view-tag class.
#
# ⚠ THIS LEAK MASKS OTHER DEFECTS. A dangling view of `s`'s buffer keeps
# printing the right answer through the `&` route precisely because the
# buffer it points at is never freed, so an `&`-rooted probe of any
# use-after-free class is green for the wrong reason (SIX Q#6).
#
# ⚠ ASan CANNOT SEE THIS. The runtime allocates from the custom
# `__gorget_current_alloc` pool. `--clones=stats live_bytes` is the
# instrument; the wired test reads it. INTENDED: live_bytes=0.
#
# The final print is the observable anchor only — the leak is not visible
# in stdout, which is exactly why this needs the clone meter.

void grow(String &s):
    int i = 0
    while i < 64:
        s = s + "0123456789012345"
        i = i + 1

void main():
    String s = "abcde"
    s = s + "f"
    grow(&s)
    print(s.len())
